Gonorrhea
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Copious purulent urethral/cervical discharge, Severe dysuria, Friable inflamed cervix, Intracellular Gram-negative diplococci

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-011
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower pelvic pain and active vaginal bleeding in the context of purulent discharge requiring urgent assessment for pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) or acute surgical abdomen.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of deep pelvic pain, copious purulent discharge, and a friable bleeding cervix. Urgent physical assessment is required to exclude life-threatening acute pelvic pathology (e.g., ectopic pregnancy, ovarian torsion) and initiate immediate therapy for severe PID to prevent systemic dissemination prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower stomach has a deep, sharp pain, it burns like fire when I pee, and I'm bleeding even though it's not my period."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 48 hours ago with severe, sharp dysuria characterized by an intense burning sensation during micturition. This has rapidly progressed to deep pelvic pain and exquisite bilateral lower abdominal discomfort over the last 12 hours. The patient reports copious, thick, and highly purulent (yellow/green) discharge from the cervical os, accompanied by new-onset intermenstrual bleeding and postcoital spotting. Denies prior history of similar symptoms.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with difficulty; bent over guarding the abdomen when walking.
Social/Safety Concerns: High distress regarding the pain and abnormal bleeding; lives alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/75 | HR: 98 | Temp: 38.2 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, flushed, diaphoretic, wincing with movement.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals marked bilateral lower quadrant tenderness with mild voluntary guarding. Normal active bowel sounds. Speculum exam (performed with consent) reveals copious purulent cervical discharge and a friable, inflamed, and severely erythematous cervix that bleeds upon minimal contact. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Copious purulent urethral/cervical discharge, Severe dysuria, Friable inflamed cervix, and Intracellular Gram-negative diplococci (pending swab results).
Environmental Observations: Clean apartment. Readily accessible bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Gonorrhea (complicated by acute Pelvic Inflammatory Disease) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Negative urine hCG test performed during the visit.

Chlamydia trachomatis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sheer volume and hyper-purulent (green/yellow) nature of the discharge, which is classically more severe in gonococcal infections, though co-infection is highly probable.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral (rather than strictly right-sided) pain, presence of purulent discharge, and a friable cervix.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe pelvic pain syndrome characterized by copious purulent discharge, high fever, and a friable bleeding cervix. The rapid escalation of deep pelvic pain necessitates immediate clinical evaluation to confidently rule out a surgical acute abdomen (ectopic pregnancy/appendicitis) and to institute immediate dual antibiotic therapy for severe PID. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period carried a high risk of permanent tubal scarring, pelvic abscess formation, or progression to disseminated gonococcal infection. This intervention satisfies the criteria for an urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe empiric Ceftriaxone 500mg IM stat (or urgent ED referral if unavailable in the bag) PLUS Doxycycline 100mg PO twice daily for 14 days (to cover presumptive co-infection). Provide urgent pathology request for an endocervical swab (NAAT and culture).
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the Doxycycline precisely 30 minutes after a full meal, the patient will prevent severe medication-induced nausea; avoiding this side effect will reinforce complete adherence to the prolonged 14-day course required to eradicate the infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or sexual health clinic review in 48-72 hours to assess clinical response and review NAAT/culture results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops intractable vomiting, inability to tolerate oral fluids/medications, worsening severe localized right upper quadrant pain (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis), or fever exceeding 39.5C.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending definitive NAAT swab results; clinical trajectory is dynamic.
Clinical Course: Pain and discharge should significantly improve within 48 to 72 hours of receiving the intramuscular antibiotic injection.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if the pain suddenly becomes agonizing, if you feel faint when standing, or if you begin vomiting and cannot keep the oral antibiotics down.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is an aggressive bacterial infection that has moved from the cervix into the pelvis. It requires immediate, strong dual antibiotics to completely kill the bacteria and prevent permanent scarring of the fallopian tubes.
Actionable Steps: Complete the entire course of Doxycycline, even when you feel better. Absolutely abstain from any sexual contact until 7 days after completing the full treatment course, and ensure all partners are tested.

SMS INFORMATION

Presumed Gonorrhoea/PID. Requires urgent antibiotic injection and a 14-day pill course. Abstain from sexual contact. Urgent GP follow-up in 2-3 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Take Doxycycline with food to stop nausea. Go to ED immediately if the pain becomes unbearable, you start vomiting, or develop a very high fever.
